WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.767 --> 00:00:01.768
-Our next guest is

00:00:01.768 --> 00:00:03.168
an Emmy-nominated writer
and comedian

00:00:03.168 --> 00:00:05.404
whose work you've seen on
"Detroiters," "Drunk History,"

00:00:05.404 --> 00:00:06.805
and right here on "Late Night."

00:00:06.805 --> 00:00:09.742
She's the host and star of
"The Amber Ruffin Show."

00:00:09.742 --> 00:00:12.478
New episodes are available
every Friday night on Peacock.

00:00:12.478 --> 00:00:13.980
Please welcome back
one of my favorite people,

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.816
the wonderful Amber Ruffin,
everybody.

00:00:16.816 --> 00:00:20.720
♪♪

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.456
-Whoa! Whoa!

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:24.757
Oh, hi.

00:00:24.757 --> 00:00:27.460
Whoa, whoa!

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:31.597
Seth!
-Oh, Amber, how are you?

00:00:31.597 --> 00:00:34.300
-[ Laughs ] It's weird
to be in the same room

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.002
with someone
who's not your husband

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:37.270
with no mask on.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.305
-I know. Look at us.
-Weird.

00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:40.940
-This is -- but, yeah,
this is, like,

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.142
counts as a full affair in 2020.
-Yeah.

00:00:43.142 --> 00:00:44.844
Oh, yay, finally.

00:00:44.844 --> 00:00:47.480
-Now, obviously, we're taking
advantage of our distance here,

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:48.714
but we have both --

00:00:48.714 --> 00:00:49.949
because you're doing a show
in the building

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:51.117
and I'm doing a show
in the building,

00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:52.351
we're both getting tested a lot.

00:00:52.351 --> 00:00:53.619
We should make sure,
in case anybody's watching

00:00:53.619 --> 00:00:55.321
and feels like this is --

00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:56.956
if they're feeling
a little squirrelly watching,

00:00:56.956 --> 00:00:58.491
they should know
that we're very safe.

00:00:58.491 --> 00:01:01.627
-We are tested constantly.

00:01:01.627 --> 00:01:02.762
How often are you tested?

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:04.163
I'm tested two or three times
a week.

00:01:04.163 --> 00:01:05.364
-Three times a week.

00:01:05.364 --> 00:01:06.966
I'm three times a week.
-Whoa.

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:09.135
-Yeah. Are you getting better
at the self-swab?

00:01:09.135 --> 00:01:11.604
-I'm a pro at the self-swab.
-Yeah.

00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:13.339
-'Cause once
you had your mask on all day,

00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:15.140
you got a bunch of snot going,
anyway.

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:16.843
Self-swab is easy.
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:20.546
-But when that man sticks
that thing in your nose --

00:01:20.546 --> 00:01:21.747
-They're called a doctor.
[ Laughs ]

00:01:21.747 --> 00:01:23.916
-You know,
the man with the white coat.

00:01:23.916 --> 00:01:25.752
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.
-I don't know who he is.

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:28.854
But when he sticks that thing
in my nose, that's very bad.

00:01:28.854 --> 00:01:30.222
-Now, that guy
is in the building, right?

00:01:30.222 --> 00:01:31.690
This isn't a man on the street.

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:33.993
-Oh, yeah, he's got -- he's
right outside the building.

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:35.127
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:35.127 --> 00:01:37.697
-He's always like,
"Hey, hey, come here."

00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:39.766
"Okay."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:42.034
-You are too gullible.
You are --

00:01:42.034 --> 00:01:43.703
You have too big a heart.
You think everybody --

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:45.772
-I get a lollipop,
so I'll do it.

00:01:45.772 --> 00:01:47.673
-Oh, that was the --
that was the exchange?

00:01:47.673 --> 00:01:49.208
-Mm-hmm.
-So, Amber, you are now --

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:51.410
on Friday, you will record
the third episode

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:52.846
of "The Amber Ruffin Show."
-Yeah.

00:01:52.846 --> 00:01:55.114
-And for those
who haven't seen it --

00:01:55.114 --> 00:01:56.682
or I should say,
for those who have seen it --

00:01:56.682 --> 00:01:58.084
it looks very different
than this set,

00:01:58.084 --> 00:02:00.253
but it is taking place
right here in this studio.

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:01.654
-You guys, it's the same --

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:03.055
Hey, hey.

00:02:03.055 --> 00:02:05.824
You guys, this is the same set.
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:05.824 --> 00:02:08.594
How is it launching --
obviously, we're figuring out

00:02:08.594 --> 00:02:09.962
how to do a show
during a pandemic.

00:02:09.962 --> 00:02:12.097
How is it launching a show
during a pandemic?

00:02:12.097 --> 00:02:15.267
-It's perfectly fine.

00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:20.740
Look, Jenny, our head writer
and my little best friend, and I

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.175
talk nonstop.

00:02:23.175 --> 00:02:26.178
Now, the only way that that
could possibly have been solved

00:02:26.178 --> 00:02:28.548
is if we work over Zoom.
-Right.

00:02:28.548 --> 00:02:31.050
-I don't know
how we would do this show

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.085
if we didn't work over Zoom.

00:02:33.085 --> 00:02:35.521
Because you have to say goodbye

00:02:35.521 --> 00:02:37.523
so that you can use
this same computer

00:02:37.523 --> 00:02:39.559
to write your sketches.

00:02:39.559 --> 00:02:42.294
Otherwise, we would still be
on that first call.

00:02:42.294 --> 00:02:44.464
We love to talk.
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:49.168
-And we do set aside an hour

00:02:49.168 --> 00:02:50.870
'cause we know
we're going to be like,

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.739
"Oh, my God, do you remember
that guy with the red hair?"

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:54.240
"Yes, I remember that guy."

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.242
And then that's
a whole hour gone.

00:02:56.242 --> 00:02:58.477
-Now, one of the things
that people might not know

00:02:58.477 --> 00:02:59.979
about our writer's room

00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:02.915
is that it's all of our writers
at "Late Night" share a room.

00:03:02.915 --> 00:03:04.950
-Yeah.
-And so -- and you know,

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.253
and one of the nice things is,
of course, the fact that,

00:03:07.253 --> 00:03:11.157
when 50 people share a room,
there's always good gossip.

00:03:11.157 --> 00:03:15.428
Do you miss that about the
writers being distant right now?

00:03:15.428 --> 00:03:19.265
-Yes. Who knows what's going on
with these people?

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:20.399
-Right.
-We don't have

00:03:20.399 --> 00:03:22.068
enough time together.

00:03:22.068 --> 00:03:26.472
We -- You know,
I miss people showing up

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:29.242
and wearing something
they don't normally wear.

00:03:29.242 --> 00:03:31.811
-Yeah.
-And then getting made fun of

00:03:31.811 --> 00:03:34.380
so terribly
that they want to go home,

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.115
but they can't
because they work here.

00:03:36.115 --> 00:03:37.383
-Yeah, I feel as though

00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:39.352
we have a very loving,
supportive environment.

00:03:39.352 --> 00:03:41.955
But if somebody dresses
even a little outside --

00:03:41.955 --> 00:03:43.922
not like what we dress like,

00:03:43.922 --> 00:03:46.525
but their own --
what they have established.

00:03:46.525 --> 00:03:49.862
-The tiniest bit different,
whoo, buddy.

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:52.398
You want to come to work
in shorts?

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:53.766
Go on and try it.

00:03:53.766 --> 00:03:57.069
-Now, I'm not gonna name names,
but, obviously someone...

00:03:57.069 --> 00:03:58.304
-Mike Scollins.

00:03:58.304 --> 00:04:00.540
-...showed up on Zoom
in a tank top.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.208
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:02.208 --> 00:04:05.511
One of the writers showed up
to Zoom in a tank top.

00:04:05.511 --> 00:04:08.814
And that was, no kidding,
five minutes of bits.

00:04:08.814 --> 00:04:12.285
-Yeah, five minutes.
-Solid bits on this poor guy,

00:04:12.285 --> 00:04:14.353
and his --
and he's in his house.

00:04:14.353 --> 00:04:16.121
-Yeah.
-He's not even with us.

00:04:16.121 --> 00:04:17.523
-He should be allowed to do
whatever he wants.

00:04:17.523 --> 00:04:18.591
I should note, I think, again,

00:04:18.591 --> 00:04:20.025
it speaks
to how supportive we are,

00:04:20.025 --> 00:04:22.027
the next Zoom call,

00:04:22.027 --> 00:04:24.330
Mike Karnell, another writer,
also wore a tank top.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.365
-In solidarity.
-Yeah.

00:04:26.365 --> 00:04:28.634
-[ Laughs ] It's so cute.
-It's like when Adam Sandler,

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:30.302
like, pees his own pants
in that movie.

00:04:30.302 --> 00:04:31.904
-[ Laughs ]
-It's like,

00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:34.172
"Hey, don't feel bad, kid.
We all piss ourselves."

00:04:34.172 --> 00:04:36.341
-"I pissed my pants."

00:04:36.341 --> 00:04:38.310
-You mentioned your --

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:39.579
I guess Jenny is --

00:04:39.579 --> 00:04:41.180
is she the producer of the show,
head writer of the show?

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:45.050
-Jenny is the head writer
and executive producer.

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:46.652
-And she's also a writer
for "Late Night,"

00:04:46.652 --> 00:04:48.521
a writer
that you introduced to us.

00:04:48.521 --> 00:04:51.423
And you knew Jenny back
in Second City days.

00:04:51.423 --> 00:04:55.294
-Yeah. Jenny and I did
Second City Denver together,

00:04:55.294 --> 00:04:58.097
which is a real thing
that really happened,

00:04:58.097 --> 00:05:00.032
and no one knows about it.

00:05:00.032 --> 00:05:02.368
But Second City Denver existed.

00:05:02.368 --> 00:05:03.869
Yes, there's
Second City Toronto --

00:05:03.869 --> 00:05:06.039
Is that true?
-There was for a while.

00:05:06.039 --> 00:05:08.774
-Yes, there is
Second City Las Vegas.

00:05:08.774 --> 00:05:10.877
And, yes,
there's Second City Chicago.

00:05:10.877 --> 00:05:15.547
But Second City Denver
also existed at one point.

00:05:15.547 --> 00:05:16.715
-And that's where you guys met?

00:05:16.715 --> 00:05:18.684
And did you guys click
right away?

00:05:18.684 --> 00:05:20.586
-Yeah.
We had the best time.

00:05:20.586 --> 00:05:26.325
We all lived
in this big building downtown.

00:05:26.325 --> 00:05:30.997
Friggin' just like the first
regular paycheck of your life,

00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:34.433
and, like, you live
in this nice apartment for free.

00:05:34.433 --> 00:05:35.634
It was horrible.

00:05:35.634 --> 00:05:37.136
We behaved terrible.

00:05:37.136 --> 00:05:39.305
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:39.305 --> 00:05:41.173
You also have a sidekick
on the show.

00:05:41.173 --> 00:05:43.009
-[ Gasps ] Tarik Davis.
-Tarik Davis,

00:05:43.009 --> 00:05:44.309
who I've known
for a long time --

00:05:44.309 --> 00:05:45.678
you've known longer, obviously.

00:05:45.678 --> 00:05:46.879
And one of the things --

00:05:46.879 --> 00:05:48.781
certainly,
one of the most fun parts

00:05:48.781 --> 00:05:50.583
about putting together writing
staff when I started this show

00:05:50.583 --> 00:05:53.352
was hiring people
that I'd known in the past.

00:05:53.352 --> 00:05:54.754
Are you enjoying working
with Tarik?

00:05:54.754 --> 00:05:56.656
-Yes, but you misspoke.

00:05:56.656 --> 00:05:59.925
You said that I knew Tarik
longer than you knew Tarik,

00:05:59.925 --> 00:06:01.127
but that's not true.

00:06:01.127 --> 00:06:02.327
-'Cause I knew Tarik first.

00:06:02.327 --> 00:06:04.997
-'Cause we all met --

00:06:04.997 --> 00:06:06.466
when we got to Boom Chicago,

00:06:06.466 --> 00:06:09.168
Tarik and I went to Boom Chicago
at the same time.

00:06:09.168 --> 00:06:10.836
That time when we got there,

00:06:10.836 --> 00:06:13.105
you were there visiting
for New Year's Eve.

00:06:13.105 --> 00:06:14.373
-Oh, my goodness.

00:06:14.373 --> 00:06:15.608
-So the three of us met
each other

00:06:15.608 --> 00:06:16.742
on the same day.
-All in the same night?

00:06:16.742 --> 00:06:18.010
There you go.
Look at that.

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:20.112
And now here we are.
-Yeah.

00:06:20.112 --> 00:06:22.247
-You -- You mentioned we met
in Amsterdam.

00:06:22.247 --> 00:06:23.750
-Yes.
-We both worked for a theater --

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:25.584
Tarik, as well --
called "Boom Chicago."

00:06:25.584 --> 00:06:28.554
You -- You are now married
to a Dutchman named Jan.

00:06:28.554 --> 00:06:30.189
-Yeah.
-Which sounds like, you know,

00:06:30.189 --> 00:06:31.624
a stereotype,
but it's also true.

00:06:31.624 --> 00:06:35.094
-Yeah, he loves cheese.
-[ Laughs ] He does.

00:06:35.094 --> 00:06:38.764
How did you and Jan
do through quarantine?

00:06:38.764 --> 00:06:41.733
-We've been doing great,
but we are,

00:06:41.733 --> 00:06:44.403
like, super raggedy.
-How so?

00:06:44.403 --> 00:06:50.643
-Like, I look like this because
several people stopped me

00:06:50.643 --> 00:06:55.047
and shook dirt off me
and, like, primped me up,

00:06:55.047 --> 00:06:57.183
but, naturally,
I'm a raggedy dude.

00:06:57.183 --> 00:06:59.185
Like, my husband and I
both have,

00:06:59.185 --> 00:07:01.987
like, 14-year-old boy vibes.

00:07:01.987 --> 00:07:05.324
So, like, it's us just, like,
drinking

00:07:05.324 --> 00:07:08.160
and, like,
ordering a lot of food.

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:11.563
And there is no adult there
to, like, clean it up

00:07:11.563 --> 00:07:14.867
or tell us to go outside
or shower.

00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:16.235
It's -- It's pretty bad.

00:07:16.235 --> 00:07:17.804
-It's pretty bad.
-Yeah.

00:07:17.804 --> 00:07:19.939
-Now, obviously it's very --
one of the things that I think

00:07:19.939 --> 00:07:21.006
people are very excited about
your show --

00:07:21.006 --> 00:07:22.809
it's very rare
for a black woman

00:07:22.809 --> 00:07:24.343
not just to write
for a late-night show,

00:07:24.343 --> 00:07:25.878
but now to have
her own late-night show.

00:07:25.878 --> 00:07:27.913
But the think the real
trailblazing is,

00:07:27.913 --> 00:07:29.181
I think
it's probably safe to say

00:07:29.181 --> 00:07:30.949
you're the first black woman
to have a talk show

00:07:30.949 --> 00:07:32.217
who also speaks Dutch.

00:07:32.217 --> 00:07:33.585
-[ Laughs ]
-Do you want to say anything --

00:07:33.585 --> 00:07:36.455
say anything to
our Dutch viewers real quick?

00:07:36.455 --> 00:07:40.960
-[ Speaking Dutch ]

00:07:42.895 --> 00:07:45.765
-[ Laughs ]
-[ Speaking Dutch ]

00:07:48.968 --> 00:07:50.403
-That was great.
Thank you.

00:07:50.403 --> 00:07:53.272
-So, them -- they're like,
"Ohh, she -- her Dutch is bad."

00:07:53.272 --> 00:07:55.374
[ Laughter ]

00:07:55.374 --> 00:07:58.043
-"Still, like,
I guess we can't be picky.

00:07:58.043 --> 00:07:59.645
I guess we can't --
we have to --

00:07:59.645 --> 00:08:01.146
we have to take what we get."

00:08:01.146 --> 00:08:05.651
Hey, so, you provided a great
service not just to our show,

00:08:05.651 --> 00:08:10.155
but I think to our viewers
after the George Floyd murder.

00:08:10.155 --> 00:08:11.457
You opened our show --

00:08:11.457 --> 00:08:12.992
we were still remote
at the time --

00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:14.360
for a whole week,

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:16.662
telling stories of
your interactions with police.

00:08:16.662 --> 00:08:18.130
I think it was very eye-opening
to people,

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:19.765
not just
that you had the one story,

00:08:19.765 --> 00:08:21.633
but you had multiple stories.

00:08:21.633 --> 00:08:23.468
And -- And you pointed out,
on the last day,

00:08:23.468 --> 00:08:25.003
that you had more to tell.

00:08:25.003 --> 00:08:29.007
Were you surprised at how
surprised people were by that?

00:08:29.007 --> 00:08:31.777
-I guess so.
I was surprised.

00:08:31.777 --> 00:08:33.278
I thought everybody knew that,

00:08:33.278 --> 00:08:38.884
but it makes sense now
that I realize they did not.

00:08:38.884 --> 00:08:42.120
But, you know, people went off
the hook because,

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:44.590
look, people
have been saying this.

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:46.525
And if folks
didn't realize that,

00:08:46.525 --> 00:08:49.195
then what they have

00:08:49.195 --> 00:08:52.464
is an unwillingness to believe
black people when they say,

00:08:52.464 --> 00:08:54.667
"Hey, this [bleep] is crazy."

00:08:54.667 --> 00:08:57.270
So people have been saying it.

00:08:57.270 --> 00:08:59.204
Now they're getting
the hang of it.

00:08:59.204 --> 00:09:00.306
There you go.

00:09:00.306 --> 00:09:02.241
-You're providing
another service, as well,

00:09:02.241 --> 00:09:06.112
which I should note is a little
more on the humorous side

00:09:06.112 --> 00:09:09.515
than what you shared
on the George Floyd week.

00:09:09.515 --> 00:09:12.184
-Yeah.
-Which is you and your sister,

00:09:12.184 --> 00:09:15.454
who grew up
in Omaha, Nebraska --

00:09:15.454 --> 00:09:19.458
basically, you just wrote
a series of true stories

00:09:19.458 --> 00:09:20.659
that she had written down
over the years

00:09:20.659 --> 00:09:21.727
of things that happened to her.

00:09:21.727 --> 00:09:23.062
And the title of the book is?

00:09:23.062 --> 00:09:25.130
-"You'll Never Believe
What Happened to Lacey."

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:28.133
[ Both laugh ]
And it's true.

00:09:28.133 --> 00:09:32.271
Lacey is just, like,
very, very tiny.

00:09:32.271 --> 00:09:38.176
She's, like, exactly this --
me, except smaller and cuter.

00:09:38.176 --> 00:09:42.281
So people really think
that she is to be messed with,

00:09:42.281 --> 00:09:43.649
but she's not.

00:09:43.649 --> 00:09:46.085
So she just writes down

00:09:46.085 --> 00:09:51.089
everything that is a crazy,
hilarious, racist story.

00:09:51.089 --> 00:09:52.891
And she's done this for years
and years and years.

00:09:52.891 --> 00:09:54.960
And you know,
if you're a black lady at work,

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:58.463
you have to do that for work
on a regular basis,

00:09:58.463 --> 00:10:00.466
but not me --
if you do comedy...

00:10:00.466 --> 00:10:02.434
[ Light laughter ]

00:10:02.434 --> 00:10:05.270
But she, you know,
had been doing that for years.

00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:08.373
And then we decided

00:10:08.373 --> 00:10:13.846
to just put all of
the funniest stories in a book.

00:10:13.846 --> 00:10:17.983
And there were enough
for a whole book.

00:10:17.983 --> 00:10:20.753
Just the funny ones
were enough for a book.

00:10:20.753 --> 00:10:24.923
-And this is just ones that
specifically happened to Lacey.

00:10:24.923 --> 00:10:28.027
-It's just racist stories
that have happened to Lacey

00:10:28.027 --> 00:10:31.463
that you would never,
ever, ever believe.

00:10:31.463 --> 00:10:33.231
-And when will -- when is
the book gonna be out?

00:10:33.231 --> 00:10:34.867
When are we...
-The book comes out

00:10:34.867 --> 00:10:37.203
January the 12th.

00:10:37.203 --> 00:10:42.041
And I am 79% sure that
that date is right.

00:10:42.041 --> 00:10:43.575
-[ Laughs ]

00:10:43.575 --> 00:10:45.678
Either way, people can preorder
something like that.

00:10:45.678 --> 00:10:47.446
And the date that I think we can
both agree on

00:10:47.446 --> 00:10:51.283
is Friday is the airing of a new
"Amber Ruffin Show."

00:10:51.283 --> 00:10:55.754
Amber, it is just always a
delight to spend time with you.

00:10:55.754 --> 00:10:57.088
Thanks for being here.
-Good, 'cause I'm always here.

00:10:57.088 --> 00:11:00.292
-[ Laughs ] You are always here.
It's still really nice.

00:11:00.292 --> 00:11:02.395
New episodes of
"The Amber Ruffin Show"

00:11:02.395 --> 00:11:03.895
are available
Friday nights on Peacock.

00:11:03.895 --> 00:11:04.897
We'll be right back.

